WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:06.720
Of the world’s eight largest economies, five&nbsp;
command nuclear weapons — the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:10.000
United Kingdom, China, India, and France.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:16.240
Another two — Germany and Italy&nbsp;
— host American nuclear weapons.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:21.920
Only one, the third-largest, has&nbsp;
neither built, nor borrowed them: Japan.

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:26.240
But Japan doesn’t just object to nuclear war.

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:30.240
Among East Asian countries, only Taiwan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:36.640
employs fewer active troops — though just&nbsp;
marginally, and yet with 1/5th the population.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:40.960
When you include reserve and paramilitary members,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:46.880
Japan’s armed forces are nearly six times&nbsp;
smaller than the next closest East Asian country.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.920
Of course, number of troops is&nbsp;
but one component of defense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:56.480
and no single figure can possibly&nbsp;
capture the overall strength of a nation.

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:01.520
Japan is certainly not “weak” by global standards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:08.880
Its neighbors, however, are exceptionally&nbsp;
strong, and its region especially precarious.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:16.000
This bar represents the total defense expenditure&nbsp;
in East Asia from 1994 to the present.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:25.360
During the 90s, Japan alone commanded nearly 40%&nbsp;
of the pie. Then, for all but one of the next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:31.840
20 years, its share declined, until&nbsp;
it ended up at just 10.7% in 2019.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:36.080
The missing context, you might argue, is China.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:41.920
Add it to the mix and it may seem as though&nbsp;
Japan’s defense expenditure hasn’t so much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:47.360
fallen as China’s has exploded —&nbsp;
dwarfing all others by comparison.

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:53.040
But an even closer examination&nbsp;
shows that despite this increase,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:58.400
Russia and South Korea have managed&nbsp;
to keep up — whereas Japan has not.

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:06.160
Today, the country finds itself a mere stone's&nbsp;
throw away from not one but three nuclear powers.

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:13.760
Since 1958, its fighter jets have been scrambled&nbsp;
over 30,000 times — a large and increasing portion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:18.880
of which have occurred in the last 20 years&nbsp;
in response to Chinese and Russian incursions.

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:24.640
And when North Korea fired a ballistic&nbsp;
missile directly above a Japanese island&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:29.520
home to 5 million people in&nbsp;
2017, many began to wonder…

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:33.360
Is Japan’s laudable devotion to Pacifism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:36.560
compatible with the challenges&nbsp;
of the 21st Century?

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:41.920
Sponsored by CuriosityStream and&nbsp;
Nebula — where you can watch the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:44.880
exclusive bonus video which accompanies this one.

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:49.840
The simplest measure of military&nbsp;
strength is expenditure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:56.000
It stands to reason that the more a nation spends,&nbsp;
the better prepared it’s likely to be for war.

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:01.520
From this perspective, Japan seems&nbsp;
quite formidable — especially when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:05.120
the two outliers — the U.S.&nbsp;
and China — are excluded.

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:09.760
It sits in the same league as&nbsp;
Germany, France, and South Korea.

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:13.840
But there are a number of&nbsp;
reasons expenditure alone can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:18.320
be misleading — and that’s perhaps never&nbsp;
been more true than in the case of Japan.

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:24.000
First, the most important&nbsp;
question is on what does it spend?

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:27.520
This is its 2021 defense budget.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:32.720
The single largest component&nbsp;
— 43% — is maintenance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:38.080
This is a broad category that includes&nbsp;
everything from healthcare to education,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:44.080
and fuel. But roughly half this money&nbsp;
is spent on just one thing: repair.

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:50.400
As the most earthquake-prone country on&nbsp;
earth, Japan simply has more things to fix.

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:57.520
In 2011, the Tohoku earthquake and subsequent&nbsp;
tsunami became the 2nd most costly natural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:04.080
disaster in recorded history — totaling somewhere&nbsp;
between 2 and 3 hundred billion US dollars.

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:09.920
An estimated 700,000 buildings were at&nbsp;
least partially destroyed in the process.

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:14.640
The third most expensive&nbsp;
disaster, another earthquake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:17.760
also occurred in Japan — 16 years earlier.

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:21.920
Because its defense budget&nbsp;
is for the most part fixed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:24.560
this leaves much less room for everything else.

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:31.680
Research, development, and equipment procurement&nbsp;
each comprise less than 4% of the overall budget.

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:39.120
The second most important question when it&nbsp;
comes to defense spending is: for how much?

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:42.880
Here Japan is at a severe disadvantage.

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:49.040
In 1976, its Prime Minister banned&nbsp;
the sale of military hardware to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:53.520
any nation under any circumstances&nbsp;
as part of its commitment to peace.

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:59.040
That left Japanese defense companies&nbsp;
with exactly one potential buyer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:05.280
and not just any buyer, but a defense company’s&nbsp;
least favorite kind: a staunch Pacifist.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:10.000
While American contractors like&nbsp;
Lockheed Martin make nearly all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:16.720
their money from defense, of Japan’s 10&nbsp;
largest, only two make more than 3% from it.

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:22.000
Why bend backward trying to please one&nbsp;
very picky customer, while tarnishing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:27.280
one’s reputation when there are millions of&nbsp;
enthusiastic consumer electronics customers?

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:33.840
Banned from selling overseas, its companies&nbsp;
devoted few resources to their defense divisions.&nbsp;

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:37.600
This disinterest, in turn, discouraged investment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:40.880
from the government — making&nbsp;
them even less competitive.

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:44.800
For a country famous for manufacturing, its arms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:48.960
contractors have fared remarkably&nbsp;
poorly in international rankings.

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:54.880
Japan, in other words, was forced to either&nbsp;
buy expensive equipment domestically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.880 --> 00:06:00.720
or import expensive equipment from abroad —&nbsp;
making it more dependent on others in the process.

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:09.120
After 40 years, the export ban was finally&nbsp;
lifted in 2014. Under the new rules, sales&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:14.640
are allowed so long as their nature is purely&nbsp;
defensive and quote “contribute to world peace”.

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:22.400
Despite this, Japan lost to France in 2016, and&nbsp;
just now, the U.S., in selling submarines to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:28.560
Australia. Its lack of infrastructure meant&nbsp;
it could only promise one submarine a year.

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:32.400
And while its first deal has since been struck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:36.080
other countries will still&nbsp;
have a 40-year head start.

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:43.040
Its equipment may have a reputation for high&nbsp;
quality, but it also has one for high prices.

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:48.080
Now, earthquakes and overpriced&nbsp;
equipment are significant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:55.440
obstacles — but they are just obstacles. Given&nbsp;
the requisite effort, political will, and time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:58.160
Japan could, in theory, overcome them.

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:02.560
Unfortunately, it faces another, much larger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:06.720
challenge. One for which&nbsp;
no law could possibly fix.

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:15.920
War requires soldiers. Soldiers are made of&nbsp;
humans. And Japan is rapidly running out.

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:22.480
For a country to maintain its population — to&nbsp;
neither grow, nor shrink — the average woman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:28.080
needs to have just slightly over two children&nbsp;
in her lifetime — one to replace each parent.

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:33.680
China fell below this “replacement&nbsp;
level” of fertility in 1991.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:38.160
Russia in ‘89. And Vietnam in ninety-nine.

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:44.800
The world average is trending downwards,&nbsp;
which is to say, most of the world is aging.

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:51.680
Japan — with a fertility rate of just 1.3&nbsp;
children per 2 adults — is no exception.

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:55.200
The only difference is that it began this process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.200 --> 00:08:00.320
way, way earlier — about 20 years&nbsp;
before the rest of East Asia.

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:06.480
China’s population decline is just&nbsp;
getting started, but in the last 10 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:10.320
Japan has already lost 1.5 million people.

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:17.040
During this decade, it will lose another&nbsp;
8 million. In the 2030s, 10 million more.

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:24.400
It doesn’t just lack people — it lacks the right&nbsp;
kind — those young enough to fight for the nation.

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:33.200
By 2050, 40% of the country will be aged over&nbsp;
65. Setting aside the economic consequences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:38.160
this will bring, first and foremost, this&nbsp;
creates a simple problem of manpower.

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:42.160
The broader labor shortage makes&nbsp;
it difficult for the government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:46.640
to compete with the generous wages and&nbsp;
benefits offered by private employers.

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:53.040
The country hasn’t been able to find&nbsp;
enough soldiers since 2014. In 2018,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:59.840
it was forced to lower its standards — increasing&nbsp;
the maximum age for new recruits from 26 to 32.

00:09:00.560 --> 00:09:07.520
As a result, the average age of its soldiers is&nbsp;
now 35 — a full decade older than the rest of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:14.800
East Asia. Even if these soldiers could make up&nbsp;
for youthful strength with wisdom or experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:21.920
they face a different kind of problem. In&nbsp;
short: too many chiefs and not enough Indians.

00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:25.040
Most places in the world solve&nbsp;
this problem the same way:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:30.400
If too few people voluntarily sign up,&nbsp;
the government will simply force them.

00:09:31.120 --> 00:09:37.520
Conscription is widespread across East&nbsp;
Asia. South Korea takes a roughly 20-month&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.520 --> 00:09:40.960
chunk out of the lives of its&nbsp;
young men. Until recently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:44.960
those in Taiwan had to put their&nbsp;
lives on hold for around a year.

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:52.160
But political and cultural opposition ensure the&nbsp;
Japanese armed forces will never have this option.

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:57.680
And while it’s true that technology&nbsp;
can help supplement human forces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:02.400
military strength is relative and its&nbsp;
neighbors will have no harder time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:07.600
acquiring or developing new weapons and&nbsp;
capabilities — in fact, the opposite.

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:14.880
In truth, no one knows how prepared&nbsp;
Japan is for conflict — not even Japan.

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:21.520
The very nature of war is such that an&nbsp;
outcome is never truly known until it actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:28.080
occurs. As a Pacifist nation, Japan has no&nbsp;
modern data points on which to extrapolate.

00:10:28.880 --> 00:10:34.160
Having never fired a bullet in combat for 70&nbsp;
straight years, and with commanders who have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:39.280
never in their life witnessed war firsthand,&nbsp;
making predictions is especially fraught.

00:10:40.080 --> 00:10:42.640
Even its equipment remains untested.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.280 --> 00:10:49.200
Some Mitsubishi-made tanks were found to lack&nbsp;
contingency space for injured soldiers. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:55.200
had been designed, built, and tested in sterile,&nbsp;
lab conditions — far removed from the battlefield.

00:10:56.160 --> 00:11:01.920
Another reason its capabilities are difficult&nbsp;
to objectively assess is deliberate obfuscation.

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:09.360
Officially, Japan has no aircraft carriers&nbsp;
— which have an offensive association — No,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:14.720
instead it operates defensive “helicopter&nbsp;
destroyers” — you be the judge.

00:11:15.600 --> 00:11:20.080
The nation’s historical aggression,&nbsp;
combined with its now longstanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.080 --> 00:11:24.240
peaceful and neutral stance both&nbsp;
constrain its available options.

00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:31.040
Behavior that might go unnoticed elsewhere is&nbsp;
met with great suspicion thanks to its past.

00:11:31.840 --> 00:11:36.160
By keeping a low profile, Japan&nbsp;
reassures the rest of the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.800 --> 00:11:43.120
Yet, by doing so, even the smallest molehill&nbsp;
of change is perceived by others as a mountain.

00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:46.960
Many, including the current government of Japan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:51.760
believe any change incremental enough to&nbsp;
avoid attracting attention from its neighbors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:56.320
would be, by definition, insufficient&nbsp;
to prepare the country for conflict.

00:11:57.040 --> 00:12:01.120
Indeed, the 21st century has&nbsp;
brought several new challenges,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:07.120
each of which would be significant on its own&nbsp;
— North Korea’s acquisition of nuclear weapons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:13.600
China’s attempted annexation of Japan’s primary&nbsp;
trade routes, and Russia’s general provocations.

00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:20.160
At the same time, Japan must overcome new&nbsp;
impediments — like its shrinking population.

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:26.000
So, why hasn’t the 3rd largest&nbsp;
economy devoted more money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:30.880
new laws, or any other measure, for&nbsp;
that matter, to strengthen its defenses?

00:12:31.760 --> 00:12:38.160
The ultimate answer has nothing at all to do with&nbsp;
its aging population, lack of combat experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.160 --> 00:12:47.040
or arms export law. The real reason is — next&nbsp;
time, in Part 2, of Japan’s Pacifist Paradox.

00:12:47.840 --> 00:12:53.680
But before we answer that question, we need&nbsp;
to take a brief detour to talk about Why Japan&nbsp;&nbsp;

